ML_PAGE("l2-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 2 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l2\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept accurately in the chapter's terms: primary vs secondary, broker vs dealer, margin, short sale.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em> the relationship exists: the economic cause, not just the result.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Connect it to the scenario: who supplies the money, who owns the security, who owes what, and who bears the risk; show the numbers.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Weigh alternatives, name a limitation or assumption, and give a justified, conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Technical Chapter 2 questions give marks for the formula, the substitution and the interpretation: compute margin or short-sale profit, then explain what it means for the investor.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate whether margin trading is beneficial to investors.</p>\n        <p>\"Margin trading allows investors to borrow money to buy stocks. It increases profits but is risky because investors may also lose money.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Describes margin but never explains the mechanism or reaches a conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Margin trading lets an investor buy securities with a combination of personal equity and borrowed funds, the securities serving as collateral. Because the investor controls a larger position than the equity contributed, favourable price moves produce proportionally larger returns on equity. The same leverage magnifies losses, because the loan must still be repaid when prices fall; margin interest reduces net returns, and a decline may trigger a margin call. Margin trading may therefore suit investors who understand and can withstand leveraged losses, but it does not automatically improve investment performance.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Mechanism + consequences on both sides + a conditional conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Securities market structures (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian manufacturing company wishes to raise RM50 million by issuing new 10-year corporate bonds. One month after issuance, an investor who originally purchased the bonds sells them to another investor through a securities market.</p>\n<p>The company's finance director claims that secondary markets are less important because the company does not receive funds when existing securities are resold.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Distinguish between money markets and capital markets, and primary and secondary markets. Classify the transactions in the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Explain the two principal economic functions of secondary markets and how they support the primary market.</p>\n<p>(c) [8 marks] Critically evaluate the finance director's statement, explaining the relationship between capital raising, liquidity and continuous security pricing.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Market classifications</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Securities markets can be classified according to the maturity of securities and whether they are newly issued or previously issued.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Market</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Money market</td>\n<td>Facilitates trading in short-term securities, generally maturing within one year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital market</td>\n<td>Facilitates trading in longer-term securities, including corporate bonds and stocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary market</td>\n<td>New securities are issued and sold, allowing issuers to raise capital</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary market</td>\n<td>Previously issued securities are traded between investors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the scenario, the company's newly issued 10-year bonds are sold in the primary capital market.</p>\n<p>The bonds are capital-market securities because their maturity exceeds one year. Their initial sale is a primary-market transaction because the company raises new financing.</p>\n<p>When the first investor subsequently resells the bonds, the transaction occurs in the secondary capital market. The nature and long-term maturity of the security remain unchanged, but the transaction is now a resale between investors.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that money versus capital markets and primary versus secondary markets are separate classification dimensions.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Functions of secondary markets</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies liquidity and continuous pricing as the two principal functions of secondary markets.</p>\n<p>First, secondary markets provide liquidity by allowing investors to sell existing securities rather than holding them until maturity or indefinitely. This can increase investors' willingness to purchase securities initially because they have a mechanism for converting their holdings into cash.</p>\n<p>Second, secondary markets provide continuous pricing through transactions between buyers and sellers. These prices help investors estimate the current market value of securities and evaluate investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>Both functions support the primary market. When investors expect securities to be easier to sell and price, they may be more willing to subscribe to new issues.</p>\n<p>This does not guarantee that every security will have a liquid secondary market; trading activity and demand still matter.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating the finance director's claim</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The director's statement is incomplete because it evaluates secondary markets solely according to whether they directly provide new financing to the issuer.</p>\n<p>It is correct that the company does not ordinarily receive the proceeds when existing bondholders sell their securities to other investors.</p>\n<p>However, the absence of direct capital raising does not make secondary markets economically unimportant.</p>\n<p>Secondary-market liquidity supports the attractiveness of newly issued securities. Investors purchasing a 10-year bond may be reluctant to commit funds if they cannot sell before maturity.</p>\n<p>Similarly, continuous market pricing helps establish a basis for evaluating securities and may inform the pricing of comparable new issues.</p>\n<p>Thus, primary and secondary markets provide complementary functions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Primary Market}=\\text{Capital Raising}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Secondary Market}=\\text{Liquidity + Price Discovery}}$$</div>\n<p>Conclusion: Although secondary-market transactions do not generally transfer fresh capital to the issuer, their liquidity and pricing functions support investor participation and the effectiveness of capital markets. Therefore, describing them as less important solely because they do not directly finance the company is unjustified.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>A secondary market contributes to capital formation indirectly by making securities more attractive to initial investors. The economic value of a market is not limited to the amount of new capital it directly raises.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Initial public offerings and investment banking (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>TechNova Sdn. Bhd., a rapidly growing private technology company, intends to raise substantial capital by selling shares to the public for the first time.</p>\n<p>Management appoints an investment bank to manage the offering. Because the issue is large, several other financial institutions are invited to participate in underwriting and distribution.</p>\n<p>(a) [8 marks] Explain the main stages and documents involved in the IPO process, including the prospectus, red herring, quiet period and roadshow.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Analyse the functions of an investment bank, underwriting syndicate and selling group in distributing a large securities issue.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Evaluate the importance of underwriting from the perspectives of the issuing company, investment bank and prospective investors.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 2</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Main stages and IPO documents</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>An initial public offering (IPO) is the first public sale of a company's shares. The chapter describes the process using underwriting, formal disclosure documents and presentations to potential investors.</p>\n<p>The IPO process can be understood through the following sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Company prepares to go public.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Investment banker and underwriting arrangements:</strong> organize promotion, issuance and distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Prospectus and preliminary red herring:</strong> provide information about the issuer and the issue.</li>\n<li><strong>Quiet period and roadshow:</strong> disclosure restrictions and presentations to investors.</li>\n<li><strong>Offering and distribution to investors.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The prospectus describes the securities issue and the issuing company. It provides information that prospective investors can use to assess the offering.</p>\n<p>The red herring is the preliminary prospectus made available during the waiting period. It provides information before the final offering documentation is completed.</p>\n<p>The quiet period is the period after the prospectus is filed when the company must restrict what it says about itself, under the chapter's U.S. regulatory framework.</p>\n<p>The roadshow consists of presentations to prospective investors. These presentations help communicate information about the business and attract interest in the securities offering.</p>\n<p>These mechanisms address the information needs of prospective investors and support distribution of the issue.</p>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks each for an accurate explanation of prospectus, red herring, quiet period and roadshow in the IPO context.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investment banker, syndicate and selling group</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains underwriting as an arrangement in which an investment bank purchases securities at an agreed price and assumes the financial risk of reselling them to public investors.</p>\n<p>The investment banker coordinates and facilitates the offering and, under this arrangement, accepts the risk associated with reselling the securities.</p>\n<p>An underwriting syndicate is formed when several institutions share the financial risk of underwriting a large issue. This prevents the underwriting exposure from being concentrated entirely in one institution.</p>\n<p>A selling group consists of other brokerage firms assisting the underwriting syndicate in distributing securities to investors.</p>\n<p>The roles are therefore distinct:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Participant</th>\n<th>Primary function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Investment banker</td>\n<td>Coordinates the offering and undertakes underwriting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Underwriting syndicate</td>\n<td>Shares financial underwriting risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selling group</td>\n<td>Helps distribute securities to investors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The chapter also identifies the tombstone as a public announcement describing the issue and the participants' roles, and describes investment-banker compensation as typically taking the form of a discount on the securities' sale price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation of underwriting</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>From the issuing company's perspective, underwriting provides access to professional expertise and assistance in raising capital. In the chapter's underwriting arrangement, the investment bank assumes the risk of reselling the issue.</p>\n<p>From the investment bank's perspective, underwriting creates an opportunity to earn compensation but also involves financial exposure if the securities cannot be distributed under favorable conditions.</p>\n<p>For prospective investors, the underwriting and offering process provides organized access to new securities and related disclosures. However, the presence of an underwriter does not guarantee that the investment is profitable or that the issuer is financially sound.</p>\n<p>The company must therefore balance the benefits of underwriting against the compensation paid to the investment bank.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Underwriting facilitates large securities offerings by combining distribution expertise with financial risk-bearing. It benefits capital raising but transfers certain risks rather than eliminating all risks associated with the investment.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Broker, dealer and alternative trading markets (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A pension fund wants to purchase 100,000 shares of an established company. It is considering execution through a traditional broker market, a dealer market or an alternative trading system.</p>\n<p>A dealer quotes the shares at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid}=\\text{RM}24.80,\\qquad \\text{Ask}=\\text{RM}25.20$$</div>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Differentiate broker markets and dealer markets by explaining how transactions occur and who acts as the counterparty.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Calculate the bid-ask spread and the gross cost of immediately purchasing 100,000 shares from the dealer. Explain the significance of the spread.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Compare the third and fourth markets described in the chapter.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend factors the pension fund should evaluate when selecting an execution mechanism.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Broker versus dealer markets</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Broker markets involve intermediaries bringing buyers and sellers together so that the parties can execute transactions with one another.</p>\n<p>The broker primarily facilitates the transaction.</p>\n<p>Dealer markets differ because a dealer, or market maker, acts as a trading counterparty. The dealer may purchase securities from a seller and subsequently offer them to another buyer.</p>\n<p>The fundamental difference is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Broker}=\\text{Facilitates the transaction}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Dealer}=\\text{Acts as a trading counterparty}}$$</div>\n<p>This distinction has economic consequences.</p>\n<p>A dealer may hold securities as inventory and therefore faces the risk that their market value changes before they can be resold.</p>\n<p>By providing a market for buying and selling securities, dealers can contribute to liquidity.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes national and regional exchanges as broker markets and Nasdaq and OTC markets as dealer markets, reflecting its historical market classification.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Bid-ask calculations</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The dealer quotes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid}=\\text{RM}24.80$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ask}=\\text{RM}25.20$$</div>\n<p>The bid is the price the dealer offers to pay when purchasing securities.</p>\n<p>The ask is the price the dealer requires when selling securities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the bid-ask spread is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Spread}=25.20-24.80$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Spread}=\\text{RM}0.40\\text{ per share}}$$</div>\n<p>The pension fund intends to purchase 100,000 shares.</p>\n<p>Assuming the dealer can fill the entire order at the quoted ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Purchase Cost}=100{,}000(25.20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}520{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>If the pension fund immediately sold the same shares back at the unchanged quoted bid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sale Proceeds}=100{,}000(24.80)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}2{,}480{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The round-trip spread cost would be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2{,}520{,}000-2{,}480{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}40{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The spread represents a trading cost to an investor crossing the dealer's quoted prices. It can also help compensate market makers for liquidity provision and inventory risk.</p>\n<p>For a large institutional order, the investor should not automatically assume that the entire quantity is available at one quoted price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Third versus fourth markets</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes the third market as institutional trading through market makers who are not members of a securities exchange.</p>\n<p>Institutions may use these arrangements to obtain lower trading costs, particularly for large transactions.</p>\n<p>The fourth market involves institutional investors dealing directly with one another, bypassing market makers.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies electronic communications networks (ECNs) as mechanisms facilitating such transactions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Third market</th>\n<th>Fourth market</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Market maker</td>\n<td>Involved</td>\n<td>Bypassed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main participants</td>\n<td>Large institutional investors</td>\n<td>Large institutional investors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trading mechanism</td>\n<td>Through non-exchange-member market maker</td>\n<td>Direct institutional trading, often electronically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economic objective</td>\n<td>Reduce transaction costs</td>\n<td>Reduce intermediation and trading costs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>(d) Recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The pension fund should evaluate the available liquidity, quoted prices, potential market impact and transaction costs.</p>\n<p>A dealer may offer immediate access to a counterparty, but a large order could face significant spread costs or insufficient depth at the quoted price.</p>\n<p>A broker market may help match the fund's order against available counterparties.</p>\n<p>An alternative trading arrangement may reduce certain intermediary costs if sufficient institutional liquidity exists.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The most appropriate execution mechanism is the one offering a suitable combination of execution reliability, liquidity and total trading cost for the fund's required transaction size, rather than whichever market has the most recognizable name.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Globalization of securities markets (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian investor is considering allocating RM100,000 to U.S. equities to diversify a portfolio currently invested entirely in Malaysian securities.</p>\n<p>The investor argues:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Foreign investments are automatically safer because they spread risk across countries.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In one hypothetical year, the U.S. stock investment appreciates by 10% in USD, but the USD depreciates by 12% against MYR.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain three advantages of international diversification.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Analyse the main risks of investing in foreign securities, emphasizing government-policy and currency exchange risks.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate the investor's return in MYR and explain the result.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the investor's statement and recommend an appropriate decision framework.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Advantages of international diversification</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies several reasons why international investments may improve portfolio diversification.</p>\n<p>First, international markets provide access to a broader selection of securities and industries. Investors are therefore not limited to opportunities available in their domestic market.</p>\n<p>Second, different economies may experience different business-cycle conditions. Returns in international markets do not necessarily move in the same direction or at the same time, potentially reducing portfolio concentration.</p>\n<p>Third, foreign markets can offer opportunities for growth and attractive investment returns, particularly in economies experiencing rapid expansion.</p>\n<p>These advantages can improve the opportunity set available to investors. However, they do not guarantee better results.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) International investment risks</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes that ordinary investment risks continue to apply when investing internationally, with government-policy and currency risks creating additional concerns.</p>\n<p>Government-policy risk arises from differences or changes in laws, taxation, labor regulation and political conditions.</p>\n<p>For example, a foreign government might introduce restrictions affecting security ownership or investment transactions.</p>\n<p>Currency exchange risk arises because foreign investments are denominated in currencies whose values can fluctuate relative to the investor's domestic currency.</p>\n<p>An investor may therefore experience a favorable return in the investment's currency but an unfavorable return after converting the proceeds back to their home currency.</p>\n<p>The chapter also notes that foreign securities markets may differ in regulatory standards.</p>\n<p>International diversification can reduce exposure to some domestic risks while introducing new sources of uncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) MYR return calculation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The U.S. equity investment appreciates by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{stock}}=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The USD depreciates against MYR by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{currency}}=-12\\%$$</div>\n<p>The exact combined home-currency return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1+R_{\\text{MYR}} = (1+R_{\\text{stock}})(1+R_{\\text{currency}})$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=(1.10)(0.88)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.968$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{MYR}}=0.968-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{MYR}}=-3.2\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For the original RM100,000 investment:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ending Value}=100{,}000(0.968)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}96{,}800}$$</div>\n<p>The investor experiences an overall loss of RM3,200 despite the underlying U.S. security increasing by 10% in USD.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates the importance of distinguishing foreign-currency security performance from the investor's home-currency return.</p>\n<p>The combined-return formula is a mathematical application of the chapter's exchange-rate-risk concept.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation and recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The claim that foreign investments are automatically safer is incorrect.</p>\n<p>International diversification may reduce the concentration of investments in one domestic economy.</p>\n<p>However, the effectiveness of diversification depends on the relationship between foreign and domestic investment returns. Broad international economic events may affect multiple markets simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Foreign investments also introduce additional exposure to currency fluctuations and government policies.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the investor should evaluate potential returns, diversification benefits, exchange-rate risks and their overall portfolio objectives before allocating capital.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: International investment should be used to improve the portfolio's risk-return characteristics where appropriate, not because foreign securities are inherently safer.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Securities regulations and trading hours (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A corporation prepares to issue securities to the public. During the process, an executive uses nonpublic information to trade shares. Separately, investors become concerned about financial disclosure, accounting reliability and whether markets are properly regulated.</p>\n<p>(a) [8 marks] Explain the primary purposes of four major U.S. securities laws discussed in Chapter 2.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Analyse why insider trading can damage investor confidence and explain the purpose of Blue Sky Laws.</p>\n<p>(c) [3 marks] State the regular U.S. trading hours given in the chapter and distinguish regular trading from extended-hours electronic trading.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate how regulation supports the functioning of securities markets without guaranteeing investment returns.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Four major securities laws</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter presents securities regulation as a framework for disclosure, supervision and investor protection.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>U.S. legislation</th>\n<th>Principal purpose stated in the chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Act of 1933</td>\n<td>Required full disclosure of information by companies issuing securities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Act of 1934</td>\n<td>Established the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) as a government regulatory body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investment Company Act of 1940</td>\n<td>Established regulation of investment companies such as mutual funds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002</td>\n<td>Tightened accounting and auditing guidelines to reduce corporate fraud</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks per law, covering identification and its principal function.</p>\n<p>Other legislation identified in the chapter includes the Maloney Act of 1938, Investment Advisers Act of 1940, Securities Acts Amendments of 1975, and Insider Trading and Fraud Act of 1988.</p>\n<p>These can be understood through their functions rather than memorizing dates without context.</p>\n<p>For example, the 1933 Act deals with securities disclosure, while Sarbanes-Oxley focuses on accounting and auditing practices.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Insider trading and Blue Sky Laws</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes insider trading as using nonpublic information about a company to profit from securities transactions.</p>\n<p>Such trading can create unfair informational advantages between market participants.</p>\n<p>For example, an executive who trades ahead of a material corporate announcement may exploit information unavailable to ordinary investors.</p>\n<p>If investors believe that securities markets systematically favor individuals with privileged information, confidence in the fairness of market transactions may deteriorate.</p>\n<p>The chapter also describes Blue Sky Laws as state-level securities regulations intended to protect investors from fraudulent securities offerings.</p>\n<p>The name reflects the concern that investors may otherwise be sold investments with little genuine economic substance.</p>\n<p>Both regulatory mechanisms support market integrity, although they address different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Trading hours</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>According to the chapter, the regular trading session for major U.S. securities exchanges and Nasdaq is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{9:30 AM-4:00 PM Eastern Time}}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter also discusses extended-hours electronic trading outside the regular session.</p>\n<p>In its historical description, extended-hours orders are filled only when matching opposing orders are available.</p>\n<p>The key distinction is that regular trading takes place within the standard exchange session, while extended-hours trading provides additional electronic trading opportunities under different trading conditions.</p>\n<p>The exact extended-hours schedules in the 2014 textbook should be treated as historical course content rather than current operating hours.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of securities regulation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Securities regulation supports financial markets by encouraging disclosure, addressing misconduct and establishing standards for market participants.</p>\n<p>Disclosure requirements help reduce informational disadvantages by providing investors with information about issuers and securities.</p>\n<p>Rules targeting insider trading and fraudulent practices aim to preserve market confidence.</p>\n<p>Accounting and auditing requirements can improve the reliability of corporate financial reporting.</p>\n<p>However, regulation does not guarantee that issuers will be successful or that investors will achieve positive returns.</p>\n<p>An accurately disclosed security can still decline in price because of deteriorating business conditions or changes in investor expectations.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Regulation aims to support transparency, integrity and investor confidence, but investors remain responsible for evaluating the financial risks of their investment decisions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Long purchases, margin trading and short selling (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor believes a stock will appreciate and considers purchasing 100 shares at RM50 each.</p>\n<p>Two alternatives are available: purchase entirely with cash or use 50% initial margin.</p>\n<p>A second investor expects the share price to decline and considers short selling 100 shares at RM50.</p>\n<p>Assume no dividends, commissions, borrowing costs or other charges unless stated.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Distinguish long purchases, margin purchases and short sales according to their mechanics and expected price direction.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] For the margin purchase, calculate the initial investor equity, loan amount and return on initial equity if the share price rises to RM65. Compare the return with a cash purchase.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] If the share price instead falls to RM35, calculate the current margin ratio and return on initial equity. Interpret the result and explain margin-call risk.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Calculate the short seller's profit if the price falls to RM35 and loss if it rises to RM65. Evaluate the major risk asymmetry of short selling.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · High priority</em></p>\n<p>This is especially important for technical examinations because it combines numerical calculations and financial reasoning.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Comparing the three securities transactions</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Transaction</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Expected price direction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Long purchase</td>\n<td>Investor buys securities and holds them</td>\n<td>Price increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td>Investor buys securities partly using borrowed funds</td>\n<td>Price increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short sale</td>\n<td>Investor borrows securities, sells them and later repurchases them</td>\n<td>Price decreases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Long purchases and margin purchases both normally benefit when the security price increases.</p>\n<p>However, a margin purchase introduces a loan obligation and magnifies the percentage effect of price movements on investor equity.</p>\n<p>A short sale benefits when the price declines because the investor hopes to repurchase the borrowed securities at a lower price.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Margin purchase and price appreciation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor purchases:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100\\text{ shares}\\times \\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Initial Position Value}=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The initial margin requirement is 50%.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Investor Equity}=5{,}000(0.50)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>The remaining financing comes from borrowing:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Loan}=5{,}000-2{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose the share price increases to RM65.</p>\n<p>The new investment value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=100(65)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}6{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The capital gain is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$6{,}500-5{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}1{,}500$$</div>\n<p>For the investor who paid entirely with cash:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{cash}}=\\frac{1{,}500}{5{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{30\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For the margin investor:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{margin}}=\\frac{1{,}500}{2{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{60\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Return on initial equity</th>\n<th>Initial equity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash purchase</td>\n<td><strong>+30%</strong></td>\n<td>RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td><strong>+60%</strong></td>\n<td>RM2,500</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The leveraged investment produces twice the percentage return because the same RM1,500 capital gain is measured against a smaller initial equity contribution.</p>\n<p>This result ignores margin interest and transaction costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Margin purchase and price decline</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>Now assume the share price falls to RM35 instead.</p>\n<p>The current security value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=100(35)=\\text{RM}3{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The debit balance remains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=\\text{RM}2{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The chapter provides the following margin formula:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Margin}=\\frac{V-D}{V}}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Margin} = \\frac{3{,}500-2{,}500}{3{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{1{,}000}{3{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{28.57\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investor's current equity has declined to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}1{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The capital loss is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3{,}500-5{,}000=-\\text{RM}1{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The return on original investor equity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{margin}}= \\frac{-1{,}500}{2{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-60\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For an entirely cash-financed purchase:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{cash}}=\\frac{-1{,}500}{5{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-30\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Return on initial equity</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash purchase</td>\n<td><strong>-30%</strong></td>\n<td>RM1,500 capital loss on RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td><strong>-60%</strong></td>\n<td>RM1,500 loss on RM2,500 equity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Interpretation: The stock falls by 30%, but the margin investor loses 60% of initial equity because the borrowed amount remains owed.</p>\n<p>A margin call may arise if the current margin ratio falls below the broker's applicable maintenance requirement.</p>\n<p>Because no maintenance margin threshold is specified in the question, it is not possible to conclude definitively that a margin call has occurred.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important: a large loss does not automatically establish a margin call without knowing the relevant requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Short selling and risk asymmetry</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The short seller borrows 100 shares and sells them at RM50.</p>\n<p>Initial sale proceeds:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(50)=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>If the stock falls to RM35, the cost of repurchasing the shares is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(35)=\\text{RM}3{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000-3{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}1{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>If the price instead rises to RM65:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Repurchase Cost}=100(65)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}6{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Profit or loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000-6{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-\\text{RM}1{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>Short selling reverses the normal sequence of buying and selling.</p>\n<p>Its central risk asymmetry arises because a stock price cannot decline below zero, but can theoretically increase without a fixed upper limit.</p>\n<p>For the example, the maximum gross price-related profit would be RM5,000 if the shares became worthless. However, the potential price-related loss has no theoretical upper limit.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Short-Sale Profit Potential Is Limited}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Short-Sale Loss Potential Is Theoretically Unlimited}}$$</div>\n<p>The short seller also remains obligated to return the borrowed shares even if the market price increases significantly.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Cash purchases, leveraged purchases and short sales generate different investor outcomes from the same market movements. Leverage increases percentage exposure, while short selling reverses the direction of profitability and introduces asymmetric price risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<p>Margin buying involves an obligation to repay borrowed money. Short selling involves an obligation to return borrowed securities.</p>\n<p>Both create risks beyond those associated with holding fully paid shares, but their financial mechanics are different.</p></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"l2s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">7 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. How to structure a high-scoring subjective answer\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s1\" title=\"1. How to structure a high-scoring subjective answer\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>How to structure a high-scoring subjective answer</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s2\" title=\"2. Full mock subjective examination\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Full mock subjective examination</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s3\" title=\"3. Model answers and marking guidance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s4\" title=\"4. Additional short-answer questions for revision\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Additional short-answer questions for revision</button></li><li data-full=\"5. How to earn marks in technical subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s5\" title=\"5. How to earn marks in technical subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>How to earn marks in technical subjective questions</button></li><li data-full=\"6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 2\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s6\" title=\"6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 2\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>What I would prioritize for Chapter 2</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Final high-level exam exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l2s-s7\" title=\"7. Final high-level exam exercise\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Final high-level exam exercise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 2 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>7 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 2 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Securities Markets and Transactions</p>\n<p>Chapter 2 is particularly suitable for technical subjective questions because it combines financial market structures, economic mechanisms, institutional responsibilities, risk analysis and numerical calculations.</p>\n<p>For example, a basic question might ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What is margin trading?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A more advanced question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>An investor purchases securities using 50% margin. Evaluate how a 30% decline in the securities' value affects the investor's equity, return and exposure to a margin call.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The second question requires more than a definition. You must explain leverage, calculate the financial consequences, identify the risks and evaluate the investment decision.</p>\n<p>This preparation follows the six learning objectives of your uploaded Chapter 2 slides, including securities markets, IPOs, brokers and dealers, international investing, regulation, and securities transactions.</p><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. How to structure a high-scoring subjective answer</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Use four levels of reasoning, particularly when the question asks you to analyse, evaluate, discuss or recommend.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Establish the financial principle:</strong> Define the concept accurately. Identify the relevant market, contract, institution or transaction.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Explain the mechanism:</strong> Show who supplies capital, who receives it, who owns the security and who bears the financial risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Apply and analyse:</strong> Use the facts and numbers in the scenario to demonstrate what happens and why.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evaluate and conclude:</strong> Identify advantages, limitations and competing risks, then provide a reasoned judgment.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example: Weak versus strong answer</h4>\n<p>Question: Evaluate whether margin trading is beneficial to investors.</p>\n<p><strong>Weak answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Margin trading allows investors to borrow money to buy stocks. It increases profits but is risky because investors may also lose money.</p>\n<p><strong>Stronger answer:</strong></p>\n<p>Margin trading allows an investor to purchase securities using a combination of personal equity and borrowed funds, with securities serving as collateral for the broker's loan. Because the investor controls a larger position relative to the equity contributed, favorable price movements can generate proportionally greater returns on equity. However, the same leverage magnifies losses because the outstanding loan must still be repaid when security prices decline. Margin interest further reduces net returns, while an unfavorable decline may trigger a margin call requiring additional collateral or liquidation. Therefore, margin trading may be suitable for investors who understand and can financially withstand leveraged losses, but it should not be regarded as a mechanism that automatically improves investment performance.</p>\n<p>The stronger answer identifies the mechanism, explains the consequences, addresses both sides of the trade-off and provides a conditional conclusion.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Full mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 6 · <strong>Total marks:</strong> 120 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 120 min</p>\n<p>Each question carries 20 marks. This is an original practice examination based on your chapter, not a verified past-year paper. The model answers and illustrative marking guidance follow the questions.</p>\n<h4>Question 1 - Securities market structures (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A Malaysian manufacturing company wishes to raise RM50 million by issuing new 10-year corporate bonds. One month after issuance, an investor who originally purchased the bonds sells them to another investor through a securities market.</p>\n<p>The company's finance director claims that secondary markets are less important because the company does not receive funds when existing securities are resold.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Distinguish between money markets and capital markets, and primary and secondary markets. Classify the transactions in the scenario.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Explain the two principal economic functions of secondary markets and how they support the primary market.</p>\n<p>(c) [8 marks] Critically evaluate the finance director's statement, explaining the relationship between capital raising, liquidity and continuous security pricing.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Initial public offerings and investment banking (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>TechNova Sdn. Bhd., a rapidly growing private technology company, intends to raise substantial capital by selling shares to the public for the first time.</p>\n<p>Management appoints an investment bank to manage the offering. Because the issue is large, several other financial institutions are invited to participate in underwriting and distribution.</p>\n<p>(a) [8 marks] Explain the main stages and documents involved in the IPO process, including the prospectus, red herring, quiet period and roadshow.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Analyse the functions of an investment bank, underwriting syndicate and selling group in distributing a large securities issue.</p>\n<p>(c) [6 marks] Evaluate the importance of underwriting from the perspectives of the issuing company, investment bank and prospective investors.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Broker, dealer and alternative trading markets (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A pension fund wants to purchase 100,000 shares of an established company. It is considering execution through a traditional broker market, a dealer market or an alternative trading system.</p>\n<p>A dealer quotes the shares at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid}=\\text{RM}24.80,\\qquad \\text{Ask}=\\text{RM}25.20$$</div>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Differentiate broker markets and dealer markets by explaining how transactions occur and who acts as the counterparty.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Calculate the bid-ask spread and the gross cost of immediately purchasing 100,000 shares from the dealer. Explain the significance of the spread.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] Compare the third and fourth markets described in the chapter.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Recommend factors the pension fund should evaluate when selecting an execution mechanism.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create</p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Globalization of securities markets (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A Malaysian investor is considering allocating RM100,000 to U.S. equities to diversify a portfolio currently invested entirely in Malaysian securities.</p>\n<p>The investor argues:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Foreign investments are automatically safer because they spread risk across countries.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In one hypothetical year, the U.S. stock investment appreciates by 10% in USD, but the USD depreciates by 12% against MYR.</p>\n<p>(a) [6 marks] Explain three advantages of international diversification.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] Analyse the main risks of investing in foreign securities, emphasizing government-policy and currency exchange risks.</p>\n<p>(c) [4 marks] Calculate the investor's return in MYR and explain the result.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate the investor's statement and recommend an appropriate decision framework.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Securities regulations and trading hours (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A corporation prepares to issue securities to the public. During the process, an executive uses nonpublic information to trade shares. Separately, investors become concerned about financial disclosure, accounting reliability and whether markets are properly regulated.</p>\n<p>(a) [8 marks] Explain the primary purposes of four major U.S. securities laws discussed in Chapter 2.</p>\n<p>(b) [5 marks] Analyse why insider trading can damage investor confidence and explain the purpose of Blue Sky Laws.</p>\n<p>(c) [3 marks] State the regular U.S. trading hours given in the chapter and distinguish regular trading from extended-hours electronic trading.</p>\n<p>(d) [4 marks] Evaluate how regulation supports the functioning of securities markets without guaranteeing investment returns.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Understand → Analyse → Evaluate</p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Long purchases, margin trading and short selling (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor believes a stock will appreciate and considers purchasing 100 shares at RM50 each.</p>\n<p>Two alternatives are available: purchase entirely with cash or use 50% initial margin.</p>\n<p>A second investor expects the share price to decline and considers short selling 100 shares at RM50.</p>\n<p>Assume no dividends, commissions, borrowing costs or other charges unless stated.</p>\n<p>(a) [4 marks] Distinguish long purchases, margin purchases and short sales according to their mechanics and expected price direction.</p>\n<p>(b) [6 marks] For the margin purchase, calculate the initial investor equity, loan amount and return on initial equity if the share price rises to RM65. Compare the return with a cash purchase.</p>\n<p>(c) [5 marks] If the share price instead falls to RM35, calculate the current margin ratio and return on initial equity. Interpret the result and explain margin-call risk.</p>\n<p>(d) [5 marks] Calculate the short seller's profit if the price falls to RM35 and loss if it rises to RM65. Evaluate the major risk asymmetry of short selling.</p>\n<p>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Model answers and marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These answers are deliberately more analytical than ordinary textbook definitions. For each question, pay attention to the connection between the financial principle, the mechanism and the resulting judgment.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 1 - Securities market structures</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Market classifications</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Securities markets can be classified according to the maturity of securities and whether they are newly issued or previously issued.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Market</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Money market</td>\n<td>Facilitates trading in short-term securities, generally maturing within one year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capital market</td>\n<td>Facilitates trading in longer-term securities, including corporate bonds and stocks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Primary market</td>\n<td>New securities are issued and sold, allowing issuers to raise capital</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secondary market</td>\n<td>Previously issued securities are traded between investors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In the scenario, the company's newly issued 10-year bonds are sold in the primary capital market.</p>\n<p>The bonds are capital-market securities because their maturity exceeds one year. Their initial sale is a primary-market transaction because the company raises new financing.</p>\n<p>When the first investor subsequently resells the bonds, the transaction occurs in the secondary capital market. The nature and long-term maturity of the security remain unchanged, but the transaction is now a resale between investors.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates that money versus capital markets and primary versus secondary markets are separate classification dimensions.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Functions of secondary markets</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies liquidity and continuous pricing as the two principal functions of secondary markets.</p>\n<p>First, secondary markets provide liquidity by allowing investors to sell existing securities rather than holding them until maturity or indefinitely. This can increase investors' willingness to purchase securities initially because they have a mechanism for converting their holdings into cash.</p>\n<p>Second, secondary markets provide continuous pricing through transactions between buyers and sellers. These prices help investors estimate the current market value of securities and evaluate investment opportunities.</p>\n<p>Both functions support the primary market. When investors expect securities to be easier to sell and price, they may be more willing to subscribe to new issues.</p>\n<p>This does not guarantee that every security will have a liquid secondary market; trading activity and demand still matter.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluating the finance director's claim</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The director's statement is incomplete because it evaluates secondary markets solely according to whether they directly provide new financing to the issuer.</p>\n<p>It is correct that the company does not ordinarily receive the proceeds when existing bondholders sell their securities to other investors.</p>\n<p>However, the absence of direct capital raising does not make secondary markets economically unimportant.</p>\n<p>Secondary-market liquidity supports the attractiveness of newly issued securities. Investors purchasing a 10-year bond may be reluctant to commit funds if they cannot sell before maturity.</p>\n<p>Similarly, continuous market pricing helps establish a basis for evaluating securities and may inform the pricing of comparable new issues.</p>\n<p>Thus, primary and secondary markets provide complementary functions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Primary Market}=\\text{Capital Raising}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Secondary Market}=\\text{Liquidity + Price Discovery}}$$</div>\n<p>Conclusion: Although secondary-market transactions do not generally transfer fresh capital to the issuer, their liquidity and pricing functions support investor participation and the effectiveness of capital markets. Therefore, describing them as less important solely because they do not directly finance the company is unjustified.</p>\n<p><strong>High-mark insight:</strong></p>\n<p>A secondary market contributes to capital formation indirectly by making securities more attractive to initial investors. The economic value of a market is not limited to the amount of new capital it directly raises.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 2 - IPO process and investment banking</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 2</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Main stages and IPO documents</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>An initial public offering (IPO) is the first public sale of a company's shares. The chapter describes the process using underwriting, formal disclosure documents and presentations to potential investors.</p>\n<p>The IPO process can be understood through the following sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Company prepares to go public.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Investment banker and underwriting arrangements:</strong> organize promotion, issuance and distribution.</li>\n<li><strong>Prospectus and preliminary red herring:</strong> provide information about the issuer and the issue.</li>\n<li><strong>Quiet period and roadshow:</strong> disclosure restrictions and presentations to investors.</li>\n<li><strong>Offering and distribution to investors.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The prospectus describes the securities issue and the issuing company. It provides information that prospective investors can use to assess the offering.</p>\n<p>The red herring is the preliminary prospectus made available during the waiting period. It provides information before the final offering documentation is completed.</p>\n<p>The quiet period is the period after the prospectus is filed when the company must restrict what it says about itself, under the chapter's U.S. regulatory framework.</p>\n<p>The roadshow consists of presentations to prospective investors. These presentations help communicate information about the business and attract interest in the securities offering.</p>\n<p>These mechanisms address the information needs of prospective investors and support distribution of the issue.</p>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks each for an accurate explanation of prospectus, red herring, quiet period and roadshow in the IPO context.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investment banker, syndicate and selling group</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter explains underwriting as an arrangement in which an investment bank purchases securities at an agreed price and assumes the financial risk of reselling them to public investors.</p>\n<p>The investment banker coordinates and facilitates the offering and, under this arrangement, accepts the risk associated with reselling the securities.</p>\n<p>An underwriting syndicate is formed when several institutions share the financial risk of underwriting a large issue. This prevents the underwriting exposure from being concentrated entirely in one institution.</p>\n<p>A selling group consists of other brokerage firms assisting the underwriting syndicate in distributing securities to investors.</p>\n<p>The roles are therefore distinct:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Participant</th>\n<th>Primary function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Investment banker</td>\n<td>Coordinates the offering and undertakes underwriting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Underwriting syndicate</td>\n<td>Shares financial underwriting risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Selling group</td>\n<td>Helps distribute securities to investors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The chapter also identifies the tombstone as a public announcement describing the issue and the participants' roles, and describes investment-banker compensation as typically taking the form of a discount on the securities' sale price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evaluation of underwriting</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>From the issuing company's perspective, underwriting provides access to professional expertise and assistance in raising capital. In the chapter's underwriting arrangement, the investment bank assumes the risk of reselling the issue.</p>\n<p>From the investment bank's perspective, underwriting creates an opportunity to earn compensation but also involves financial exposure if the securities cannot be distributed under favorable conditions.</p>\n<p>For prospective investors, the underwriting and offering process provides organized access to new securities and related disclosures. However, the presence of an underwriter does not guarantee that the investment is profitable or that the issuer is financially sound.</p>\n<p>The company must therefore balance the benefits of underwriting against the compensation paid to the investment bank.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Underwriting facilitates large securities offerings by combining distribution expertise with financial risk-bearing. It benefits capital raising but transfers certain risks rather than eliminating all risks associated with the investment.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 3 - Broker and dealer markets</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 3</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Broker versus dealer markets</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>Broker markets involve intermediaries bringing buyers and sellers together so that the parties can execute transactions with one another.</p>\n<p>The broker primarily facilitates the transaction.</p>\n<p>Dealer markets differ because a dealer, or market maker, acts as a trading counterparty. The dealer may purchase securities from a seller and subsequently offer them to another buyer.</p>\n<p>The fundamental difference is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Broker}=\\text{Facilitates the transaction}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Dealer}=\\text{Acts as a trading counterparty}}$$</div>\n<p>This distinction has economic consequences.</p>\n<p>A dealer may hold securities as inventory and therefore faces the risk that their market value changes before they can be resold.</p>\n<p>By providing a market for buying and selling securities, dealers can contribute to liquidity.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes national and regional exchanges as broker markets and Nasdaq and OTC markets as dealer markets, reflecting its historical market classification.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Bid-ask calculations</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The dealer quotes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bid}=\\text{RM}24.80$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ask}=\\text{RM}25.20$$</div>\n<p>The bid is the price the dealer offers to pay when purchasing securities.</p>\n<p>The ask is the price the dealer requires when selling securities.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the bid-ask spread is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Spread}=25.20-24.80$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Spread}=\\text{RM}0.40\\text{ per share}}$$</div>\n<p>The pension fund intends to purchase 100,000 shares.</p>\n<p>Assuming the dealer can fill the entire order at the quoted ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Purchase Cost}=100{,}000(25.20)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}520{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>If the pension fund immediately sold the same shares back at the unchanged quoted bid:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sale Proceeds}=100{,}000(24.80)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}2{,}480{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The round-trip spread cost would be:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2{,}520{,}000-2{,}480{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}40{,}000}$$</div>\n<p>The spread represents a trading cost to an investor crossing the dealer's quoted prices. It can also help compensate market makers for liquidity provision and inventory risk.</p>\n<p>For a large institutional order, the investor should not automatically assume that the entire quantity is available at one quoted price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Third versus fourth markets</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes the third market as institutional trading through market makers who are not members of a securities exchange.</p>\n<p>Institutions may use these arrangements to obtain lower trading costs, particularly for large transactions.</p>\n<p>The fourth market involves institutional investors dealing directly with one another, bypassing market makers.</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies electronic communications networks (ECNs) as mechanisms facilitating such transactions.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Third market</th>\n<th>Fourth market</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Market maker</td>\n<td>Involved</td>\n<td>Bypassed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main participants</td>\n<td>Large institutional investors</td>\n<td>Large institutional investors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trading mechanism</td>\n<td>Through non-exchange-member market maker</td>\n<td>Direct institutional trading, often electronically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economic objective</td>\n<td>Reduce transaction costs</td>\n<td>Reduce intermediation and trading costs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>(d) Recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The pension fund should evaluate the available liquidity, quoted prices, potential market impact and transaction costs.</p>\n<p>A dealer may offer immediate access to a counterparty, but a large order could face significant spread costs or insufficient depth at the quoted price.</p>\n<p>A broker market may help match the fund's order against available counterparties.</p>\n<p>An alternative trading arrangement may reduce certain intermediary costs if sufficient institutional liquidity exists.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: The most appropriate execution mechanism is the one offering a suitable combination of execution reliability, liquidity and total trading cost for the fund's required transaction size, rather than whichever market has the most recognizable name.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 4 - International securities markets</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 4</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Advantages of international diversification</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter identifies several reasons why international investments may improve portfolio diversification.</p>\n<p>First, international markets provide access to a broader selection of securities and industries. Investors are therefore not limited to opportunities available in their domestic market.</p>\n<p>Second, different economies may experience different business-cycle conditions. Returns in international markets do not necessarily move in the same direction or at the same time, potentially reducing portfolio concentration.</p>\n<p>Third, foreign markets can offer opportunities for growth and attractive investment returns, particularly in economies experiencing rapid expansion.</p>\n<p>These advantages can improve the opportunity set available to investors. However, they do not guarantee better results.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) International investment risks</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes that ordinary investment risks continue to apply when investing internationally, with government-policy and currency risks creating additional concerns.</p>\n<p>Government-policy risk arises from differences or changes in laws, taxation, labor regulation and political conditions.</p>\n<p>For example, a foreign government might introduce restrictions affecting security ownership or investment transactions.</p>\n<p>Currency exchange risk arises because foreign investments are denominated in currencies whose values can fluctuate relative to the investor's domestic currency.</p>\n<p>An investor may therefore experience a favorable return in the investment's currency but an unfavorable return after converting the proceeds back to their home currency.</p>\n<p>The chapter also notes that foreign securities markets may differ in regulatory standards.</p>\n<p>International diversification can reduce exposure to some domestic risks while introducing new sources of uncertainty.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) MYR return calculation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The U.S. equity investment appreciates by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{stock}}=10\\%$$</div>\n<p>The USD depreciates against MYR by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{currency}}=-12\\%$$</div>\n<p>The exact combined home-currency return is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1+R_{\\text{MYR}} = (1+R_{\\text{stock}})(1+R_{\\text{currency}})$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=(1.10)(0.88)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=0.968$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{MYR}}=0.968-1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{R_{\\text{MYR}}=-3.2\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For the original RM100,000 investment:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Ending Value}=100{,}000(0.968)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}96{,}800}$$</div>\n<p>The investor experiences an overall loss of RM3,200 despite the underlying U.S. security increasing by 10% in USD.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates the importance of distinguishing foreign-currency security performance from the investor's home-currency return.</p>\n<p>The combined-return formula is a mathematical application of the chapter's exchange-rate-risk concept.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation and recommendation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>The claim that foreign investments are automatically safer is incorrect.</p>\n<p>International diversification may reduce the concentration of investments in one domestic economy.</p>\n<p>However, the effectiveness of diversification depends on the relationship between foreign and domestic investment returns. Broad international economic events may affect multiple markets simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Foreign investments also introduce additional exposure to currency fluctuations and government policies.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the investor should evaluate potential returns, diversification benefits, exchange-rate risks and their overall portfolio objectives before allocating capital.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: International investment should be used to improve the portfolio's risk-return characteristics where appropriate, not because foreign securities are inherently safer.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 5 - Securities regulation and trading hours</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 5</em></p>\n<p><strong>(a) Four major securities laws</strong> [8 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter presents securities regulation as a framework for disclosure, supervision and investor protection.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>U.S. legislation</th>\n<th>Principal purpose stated in the chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Act of 1933</td>\n<td>Required full disclosure of information by companies issuing securities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Securities Act of 1934</td>\n<td>Established the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) as a government regulatory body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Investment Company Act of 1940</td>\n<td>Established regulation of investment companies such as mutual funds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002</td>\n<td>Tightened accounting and auditing guidelines to reduce corporate fraud</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Suggested marking: 2 marks per law, covering identification and its principal function.</p>\n<p>Other legislation identified in the chapter includes the Maloney Act of 1938, Investment Advisers Act of 1940, Securities Acts Amendments of 1975, and Insider Trading and Fraud Act of 1988.</p>\n<p>These can be understood through their functions rather than memorizing dates without context.</p>\n<p>For example, the 1933 Act deals with securities disclosure, while Sarbanes-Oxley focuses on accounting and auditing practices.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Insider trading and Blue Sky Laws</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The chapter describes insider trading as using nonpublic information about a company to profit from securities transactions.</p>\n<p>Such trading can create unfair informational advantages between market participants.</p>\n<p>For example, an executive who trades ahead of a material corporate announcement may exploit information unavailable to ordinary investors.</p>\n<p>If investors believe that securities markets systematically favor individuals with privileged information, confidence in the fairness of market transactions may deteriorate.</p>\n<p>The chapter also describes Blue Sky Laws as state-level securities regulations intended to protect investors from fraudulent securities offerings.</p>\n<p>The name reflects the concern that investors may otherwise be sold investments with little genuine economic substance.</p>\n<p>Both regulatory mechanisms support market integrity, although they address different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Trading hours</strong> [3 marks]</p>\n<p>According to the chapter, the regular trading session for major U.S. securities exchanges and Nasdaq is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{9:30 AM-4:00 PM Eastern Time}}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter also discusses extended-hours electronic trading outside the regular session.</p>\n<p>In its historical description, extended-hours orders are filled only when matching opposing orders are available.</p>\n<p>The key distinction is that regular trading takes place within the standard exchange session, while extended-hours trading provides additional electronic trading opportunities under different trading conditions.</p>\n<p>The exact extended-hours schedules in the 2014 textbook should be treated as historical course content rather than current operating hours.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation of securities regulation</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<p>Securities regulation supports financial markets by encouraging disclosure, addressing misconduct and establishing standards for market participants.</p>\n<p>Disclosure requirements help reduce informational disadvantages by providing investors with information about issuers and securities.</p>\n<p>Rules targeting insider trading and fraudulent practices aim to preserve market confidence.</p>\n<p>Accounting and auditing requirements can improve the reliability of corporate financial reporting.</p>\n<p>However, regulation does not guarantee that issuers will be successful or that investors will achieve positive returns.</p>\n<p>An accurately disclosed security can still decline in price because of deteriorating business conditions or changes in investor expectations.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Regulation aims to support transparency, integrity and investor confidence, but investors remain responsible for evaluating the financial risks of their investment decisions.</p>\n<h4>Model Answer 6 - Long purchases, margin and short selling</h4>\n<p><em>20 marks · Learning Objective 6 · High priority</em></p>\n<p>This is especially important for technical examinations because it combines numerical calculations and financial reasoning.</p>\n<p><strong>(a) Comparing the three securities transactions</strong> [4 marks]</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Transaction</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Expected price direction</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Long purchase</td>\n<td>Investor buys securities and holds them</td>\n<td>Price increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td>Investor buys securities partly using borrowed funds</td>\n<td>Price increases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short sale</td>\n<td>Investor borrows securities, sells them and later repurchases them</td>\n<td>Price decreases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Long purchases and margin purchases both normally benefit when the security price increases.</p>\n<p>However, a margin purchase introduces a loan obligation and magnifies the percentage effect of price movements on investor equity.</p>\n<p>A short sale benefits when the price declines because the investor hopes to repurchase the borrowed securities at a lower price.</p>\n<p><strong>(b) Margin purchase and price appreciation</strong> [6 marks]</p>\n<p>The investor purchases:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100\\text{ shares}\\times \\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Initial Position Value}=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The initial margin requirement is 50%.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Investor Equity}=5{,}000(0.50)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>The remaining financing comes from borrowing:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Loan}=5{,}000-2{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}2{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>Suppose the share price increases to RM65.</p>\n<p>The new investment value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=100(65)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}6{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The capital gain is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$6{,}500-5{,}000$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}1{,}500$$</div>\n<p>For the investor who paid entirely with cash:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{cash}}=\\frac{1{,}500}{5{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{30\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For the margin investor:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{margin}}=\\frac{1{,}500}{2{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{60\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Return on initial equity</th>\n<th>Initial equity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash purchase</td>\n<td><strong>+30%</strong></td>\n<td>RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td><strong>+60%</strong></td>\n<td>RM2,500</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The leveraged investment produces twice the percentage return because the same RM1,500 capital gain is measured against a smaller initial equity contribution.</p>\n<p>This result ignores margin interest and transaction costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(c) Margin purchase and price decline</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>Now assume the share price falls to RM35 instead.</p>\n<p>The current security value is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$V=100(35)=\\text{RM}3{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The debit balance remains:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$D=\\text{RM}2{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The chapter provides the following margin formula:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Margin}=\\frac{V-D}{V}}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Margin} = \\frac{3{,}500-2{,}500}{3{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{1{,}000}{3{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{28.57\\%}$$</div>\n<p>The investor's current equity has declined to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}1{,}000$$</div>\n<p>The capital loss is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$3{,}500-5{,}000=-\\text{RM}1{,}500$$</div>\n<p>The return on original investor equity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{margin}}= \\frac{-1{,}500}{2{,}500}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-60\\%}$$</div>\n<p>For an entirely cash-financed purchase:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{cash}}=\\frac{-1{,}500}{5{,}000}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-30\\%}$$</div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Return on initial equity</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cash purchase</td>\n<td><strong>-30%</strong></td>\n<td>RM1,500 capital loss on RM5,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Margin purchase</td>\n<td><strong>-60%</strong></td>\n<td>RM1,500 loss on RM2,500 equity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Interpretation: The stock falls by 30%, but the margin investor loses 60% of initial equity because the borrowed amount remains owed.</p>\n<p>A margin call may arise if the current margin ratio falls below the broker's applicable maintenance requirement.</p>\n<p>Because no maintenance margin threshold is specified in the question, it is not possible to conclude definitively that a margin call has occurred.</p>\n<p>This distinction is important: a large loss does not automatically establish a margin call without knowing the relevant requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>(d) Short selling and risk asymmetry</strong> [5 marks]</p>\n<p>The short seller borrows 100 shares and sells them at RM50.</p>\n<p>Initial sale proceeds:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(50)=\\text{RM}5{,}000$$</div>\n<p>If the stock falls to RM35, the cost of repurchasing the shares is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$100(35)=\\text{RM}3{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Profit:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000-3{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RM}1{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>If the price instead rises to RM65:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Repurchase Cost}=100(65)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\text{RM}6{,}500$$</div>\n<p>Profit or loss:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5{,}000-6{,}500$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{-\\text{RM}1{,}500}$$</div>\n<p>Short selling reverses the normal sequence of buying and selling.</p>\n<p>Its central risk asymmetry arises because a stock price cannot decline below zero, but can theoretically increase without a fixed upper limit.</p>\n<p>For the example, the maximum gross price-related profit would be RM5,000 if the shares became worthless. However, the potential price-related loss has no theoretical upper limit.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Short-Sale Profit Potential Is Limited}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Short-Sale Loss Potential Is Theoretically Unlimited}}$$</div>\n<p>The short seller also remains obligated to return the borrowed shares even if the market price increases significantly.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Cash purchases, leveraged purchases and short sales generate different investor outcomes from the same market movements. Leverage increases percentage exposure, while short selling reverses the direction of profitability and introduces asymmetric price risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Examiner's key distinction:</strong></p>\n<p>Margin buying involves an obligation to repay borrowed money. Short selling involves an obligation to return borrowed securities.</p>\n<p>Both create risks beyond those associated with holding fully paid shares, but their financial mechanics are different.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Additional short-answer questions for revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are suitable for shorter subjective questions worth approximately 5-10 marks. Try answering each before revealing the model response.</p>\n<p><strong>Q1. Differentiate a public offering, rights offering and private placement.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> All three are methods of issuing securities through the primary market. A public offering distributes securities to public investors. A rights offering gives existing shareholders rights to subscribe for new shares. A private placement distributes securities to a selected group of investors rather than offering them broadly to the public. The primary distinction concerns the method and target investors of the offering.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q2. Explain the economic purpose of underwriting syndicates and selling groups.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> An underwriting syndicate shares the financial exposure created when investment banks undertake a large securities offering. A selling group helps distribute the issue to investors. The syndicate therefore emphasizes financial risk sharing, while the selling group emphasizes distribution. Together they support the sale of a large issue.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q3. Why can the bid-ask spread be considered a transaction cost?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The dealer offers to purchase securities at the bid price and sell them at the higher ask price. An investor who immediately buys and resells at unchanged quotes pays the spread through buying at the higher price and selling at the lower price. The spread is associated with liquidity provision and compensating market makers for trading and inventory risks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q4. Compare bull and bear markets and explain whether they predict every individual stock's performance.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chapter associates bull markets with generally rising prices, optimism and favorable economic conditions. Bear markets are associated with declining prices, pessimism and weaker economic conditions. These descriptions concern broad market behavior rather than guaranteed price movements for every security. Individual companies can perform differently from the overall market.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q5. Explain the role of American Depositary Receipts (ADRs) in international investing.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The chapter describes ADRs as dollar-denominated receipts representing shares of foreign companies held by depositary banks. They allow investors to obtain foreign-company exposure through securities traded in the U.S. market. However, foreign-investment and currency-related risks do not disappear merely because the receipt is denominated in USD.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6. Why can margin trading trigger forced liquidation even if an investor believes the security will eventually recover?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A margin investor controls securities partly financed with a loan. When market prices decline, the asset value and investor equity decrease while the loan remains owed. If the margin ratio falls below the applicable maintenance requirement, the broker may demand additional collateral or reduce the position. Therefore, an investor can face liquidation before their long-term market expectation materializes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q7. Derive the minimum security value required to maintain a specified margin percentage.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> This is a mathematical extension of the chapter's basic margin formula. Start with m = (V - D)/V. Rearranging gives mV = V - D, then D = V(1 - m), so V = D/(1 - m). For example, with a RM2,500 loan and a 30% maintenance requirement, the threshold security value is 2,500/0.70 = RM3,571.43. A lower value would put the margin ratio below 30%, assuming the loan and requirement remain unchanged.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q8. Explain why securities market regulation cannot guarantee investment returns.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Regulation seeks to improve disclosure, address misconduct and support market integrity. However, securities can decline in value because of legitimate changes in business performance, economic conditions and investor expectations. Regulatory protection against fraud does not convert risky securities into guaranteed-return investments.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. How to earn marks in technical subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For 20-mark questions, use the marks as an indication of the expected depth.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assessment component</th>\n<th>Illustrative marks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Correct definitions and principles</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Explanation of economic mechanisms</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application and numerical calculations</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical evaluation and justified conclusion</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is a general preparation framework, not your lecturer's official marking scheme.</p>\n<p>Some additional techniques will help.</p>\n<p>When asked to compare: Establish a shared basis of comparison, such as ownership, risk, financing, liquidity or investor objectives. Avoid providing two unrelated definitions.</p>\n<p>When asked to analyse: Explain the cause-and-effect mechanism. For margin trading, establish why falling asset value reduces investor equity disproportionately.</p>\n<p>When asked to evaluate: Assess both advantages and disadvantages, identify assumptions, and conclude which option is preferable under the scenario.</p>\n<p>When asked to calculate: Show the formula, substitution, answer and financial interpretation. A percentage without explanation may demonstrate arithmetic, but not understanding.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. What I would prioritize for Chapter 2</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Highest priority - Margin trading and short selling</p>\n<p>Be able to calculate initial equity, margin percentage, return on invested capital, short-sale profits and losses, and explain leverage and margin-call risks.</p>\n<p>High priority - Primary and secondary markets</p>\n<p>Understand the flow of financing, liquidity and continuous pricing. Practise questions asking whether secondary markets are economically necessary.</p>\n<p>High priority - IPOs and underwriting</p>\n<p>Explain how the IPO process works and distinguish investment bankers, underwriting syndicates and selling groups.</p>\n<p>High priority - Brokers, dealers and trading mechanisms</p>\n<p>Distinguish agent and principal roles, calculate bid-ask spreads and explain third- and fourth-market arrangements.</p>\n<p>Important - International diversification</p>\n<p>Explain diversification opportunities and government-policy and exchange-rate risks. Understand why home-currency returns may differ from foreign-currency returns.</p>\n<p>Important - Securities regulation</p>\n<p>Learn each major law by its function and explain why disclosure, market supervision and insider-trading restrictions matter.</p>\n<p>These priorities reflect the chapter's potential for technical and higher-order questions, not confirmed exam weightings.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l2s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Final high-level exam exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Evaluate the following statement:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Because margin trading allows investors to earn higher percentage returns, while short selling allows profits during falling markets, investors should routinely use both strategies to maximize their investment performance.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A high-scoring response should challenge the assumptions underlying the statement.</p>\n<p>Margin trading increases the size of the investment position relative to initial equity, magnifying favorable and unfavorable outcomes. It also creates interest expenses and margin-call exposure.</p>\n<p>Short selling creates opportunities to profit when prices decline, but its gains are limited by the stock price's lower bound of zero, while price-related losses are theoretically unlimited.</p>\n<p>The existence of a profit opportunity does not make a strategy suitable for every investor.</p>\n<p>A complete evaluation should therefore examine the investor's market expectations, financial capacity, tolerance for losses and the obligations created by each transaction.</p>\n<p>The fundamental Chapter 2 relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{ \\text{Investment Outcome} = f(\\text{Market Movement, Position Structure, Financing, Costs}) }$$</div>\n<p>The deepest lesson is that two investors trading exactly the same security can experience very different profits, losses and risks because of how their transactions are structured.</p>\n<p>That is the level of understanding to demonstrate in a technical subjective examination.</p>\n<p>Source note: Based on Chapter 2 - Securities Markets and Transactions (Pearson, 2014). The source's U.S. regulations, institutional market descriptions and trading-hour information are treated as historical textbook content. The cases, calculations and marking allocations are practice material, not verified past-exam questions or statements of current Malaysian regulatory requirements.</p></div></details></div></details>");
